# Astral contribution policy.
#
# `astral contribute database` reads this file from the repository before it
# sends anything. It is the repository's answer to the question the command
# asks: is a database submission welcome, and what may it contain?
#
# Anything not permitted here is dropped locally, before it leaves the machine.

# Whether submissions are being accepted at all.
accept yes

# Where a submission lands.
#
# A pull request, because that is what a submission is: a file the project can
# read, review as a diff, and merge in one click. An issue would be a thread
# somebody has to dig the data back out of.
#
# Contributed databases land in this directory and are compiled into the
# knowledge base at build time, so merging one is all it takes for everyone to
# get it.
method pull-request
path contrib/databases

# A service that accepts submissions from anyone, with no account and no
# browser. If the project runs one, put its URL here and every installed copy
# starts using it without needing an update.
# endpoint https://example.invalid/astral/contributions

# Record kinds that may be sent. Everything else is stripped.
#
# These describe what code means, not who wrote it or where it lived:
#   sig    a function body's fingerprint and the name someone gave it
#   proto  a function's real prototype
#   verb   a library call and the purpose it implies
#   idiom  a pair of calls and the purpose they imply
#   lit    text a function contains and the purpose it implies
#   role   how a value is used and what to call it
#   note   an explanation to attach where something appears
allow sig
allow proto
allow verb
allow idiom
allow lit
allow role
allow note

# Records containing any of these never leave the machine, whatever their kind.
# A path or an address says something about a person rather than about code.
deny /Users/
deny /home/
deny C:\
deny @

# The largest submission accepted, in records.
limit 200000

# Shown when a submission is refused, or with --dry-run.
message Only what a name means travels: no paths, no addresses, no file names.
